A||||4||ToddAsh|| Brake Pads- Recommendations||||1||
Z||000000||ToddAsh||04-23-2001||05:47 PM|||| Well I'm ready to take off my original pads and I'd like to know whats recommended.Carbo-Metallic from the Fiero Store? I don't race just would like a good pad. Stock?,Pep Boys? Put Metal on my other car and they squeal like pigs. Thanks for tips. Todd||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||04-23-2001||05:57 PM|||| Use a good stock semi metallic replacement pad. I have used the carbon metallic pads on some of my GM cars and they stop well in warm weather but as soon as it gets cold outside they suck. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||04-23-2001||06:50 PM||||I have been very happy with Carbotech Super Street pads. ( <A HREF="http://www.carbotecheng.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.carbotecheng.com/</A> )  They are designed for high-performance street driving, not racing or hardcore autocross.  At $110 per axle they are rather pricey, but they do stop well.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||04-23-2001||09:46 PM||||I've got raybestos prostop on the front. (Where most of the braking is done,,,) They work fine for me. I don't race, I do drive hard. They have around 30,000 on them and are still doing fine.<P>I just did the rear pads with an "off brand" to see how they are. So far they work fine. (My old rear were ok but I had a caliper slide bind and cause a rotor warp.... They had over 25,000 on them.)<P>I only use a semimet on street driven cars. They will usually take plenty of abuse. They aren't the greatest for race app's like SCCA. You'll want really good pads for that. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wannabe||04-23-2001||10:47 PM||||KVR performance offers and excellent all around product at a good price. Carbotech is also very good, but definitely pricey. They are better suited for people who run their cars on the track occasionally.||||reg||1
